Shade the border of a flat selection

The border of a selected item takes its pen from the colour that fills
the shape. A flat selection appearance fills the whole shape with that
one colour, so the border carries no contrast and no edge appears. The
box for the bordered edges then has no effect, and the value Raised
takes the flat fill as well.

The pen now takes the standard border shade of the selection colour
while the fill is flat. The edge stands against the fill, so the box
acts for every appearance. A selection with a gradient keeps the pen of
before, and its paint does not move.

The change sits on the branch work/style-paint of the local tree, as commit 15 of 21.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/qt6/style/qtcurve_primitive.cpp b/qt6/style/qtcurve_primitive.cpp
index dc48ea43..2c4dabab 100644
--- a/qt6/style/qtcurve_primitive.cpp
+++ b/qt6/style/qtcurve_primitive.cpp
@@ -867,8 +867,16 @@ Style::drawPrimitivePanelItemViewItem(PrimitiveElement,
                                   false, opts.selectionAppearance,
                                   WIDGET_SELECTION, false);
                 if (opts.borderSelection) {
+                    // A flat fill covers the whole shape, so the pen takes
+                    // the standard border shade to keep the contrast.
+                    QColor borderCol(color);
+                    if (qtcIsFlat(opts.selectionAppearance)) {
+                        QColor cols[TOTAL_SHADES + 1];
+                        shadeColors(color, cols);
+                        borderCol = cols[QTC_STD_BORDER];
+                    }
                     pixPainter.setBrush(Qt::NoBrush);
-                    pixPainter.setPen(color);
+                    pixPainter.setPen(borderCol);
                     pixPainter.drawPath(buildPath(border, WIDGET_SELECTION,
                                                   ROUNDED_ALL, radius));
                 }
